# =============================================================================
#  uploads/ - NEVER EXECUTE ANYTHING IN HERE
# =============================================================================
#
# Everything in this directory arrived from outside: product images, seller
# documents, chat attachments, review photos, bank-transfer receipts, bulk-upload
# CSVs. Several of the endpoints that write here are reachable without an admin
# session, and one of them (cart/send_bank_receipt) was reachable with no session
# at all.
#
# Nothing currently lets a .php file land here - every upload path goes through
# CodeIgniter's Upload library with an extension-and-MIME whitelist, and
# allowed_media_types() (application/config/eshop.php) lists no executable type.
# This file is not fixing a live hole; it is making sure the next change cannot
# open one. The gap between "an attacker can write a file here" and "an attacker
# can run code on this server" is exactly one added extension in a settings
# screen, one new upload endpoint that skips the library, or one MIME-confusion
# bug in the library itself. That gap should not be a single mistake wide.
#
# Three independent guards, because which one applies depends on how PHP is wired
# on the host and the site has moved hosts before:

# 1. Take the handler away from every extension PHP is ever mapped to. This is
#    what defeats the classic "shell.php.jpg" and "shell.phtml" tricks, and also
#    covers .phar, which is executable and often forgotten.
<FilesMatch "(?i)\.(php|php[0-9]?|phtml|pht|phps|phar|cgi|pl|py|jsp|asp|aspx|shtml|htaccess)$">
    <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
        Require all denied
    </IfModule>
    <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
        Order allow,deny
        Deny from all
    </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>

# 2. Unbind the interpreters themselves, so even a file this server would
#    normally hand to PHP is served as bytes instead of run.
<IfModule mod_php.c>
    php_flag engine off
</IfModule>
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
    RemoveHandler .php .php3 .php4 .php5 .php7 .php8 .phtml .pht .phar .cgi .pl .py
    RemoveType .php .php3 .php4 .php5 .php7 .php8 .phtml .pht .phar
    # Anything left unrecognised is a download, not a document to interpret.
    AddType text/plain .php .php3 .php4 .php5 .php7 .php8 .phtml .pht .phar .cgi .pl .py
</IfModule>

# 3. No CGI, and no directory listing of what other people have uploaded.
Options -ExecCGI -Indexes

# Uploaded SVG is an HTML document as far as a browser is concerned - it can carry
# <script> - and it IS an allowed image type in some flows. Served with a sandbox
# and a null CSP it cannot run script or reach the network even if opened directly.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    <FilesMatch "(?i)\.(svg|svgz|xml|html?|xhtml)$">
        Header always set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; sandbox"
        Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
        Header always set Content-Disposition "attachment"
    </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>
